#
#  CMake itself
#
{
   CMake Binary Leak
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,possible
   fun:malloc
   obj:*/bin/cmake
   obj:*/bin/cmake
   ...
}
#
#  Coverage instrumentation
#
#  The nightly debug builds compile with code_coverage=1, so every translation
#  unit registers its counters with the LLVM coverage runtime from an
#  initialiser. The runtime keeps the registration for the process lifetime and
#  never frees it, so every block is still reachable. Anchored on the runtime's
#  own frames, so an allocation that merely passes through the loader still
#  surfaces.
#
{
   LLVM coverage instrumentation registration
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:malloc
   fun:llvm_gcov_init
   fun:__llvm_gcov_init
   ...
}
#
#  glibc atexit handler registration
#
#  A translation unit with a namespace-scope object registers its destructor
#  with __cxa_atexit when it loads, and glibc allocates the handler node for the
#  lifetime of the process, so the nodes are still reachable rather than lost.
#
{
   glibc atexit handler registration
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:calloc
   fun:__new_exitfn
   fun:__internal_atexit
   fun:__cxa_atexit
   ...
}
#
#  Catch2 reporter output stream
#
#  The test binary opens its XML report file once, and the stream keeps its
#  buffer and its FILE until the process exits, which is after the reporter has
#  flushed. Anchored on the reporter's own stream type, so a file the tests open
#  themselves still surfaces.
#
{
   Catch2 reporter file stream
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   fun:_ZN5Catch6Detail12_GLOBAL__N_110FileStreamC1E*
   ...
}
#
#  The shell of the script tests
#
#  The end-to-end and install tests are bash scripts, so memcheck runs bash
#  itself and follows it into the compass it starts. bash keeps its variables,
#  parsed commands and hash tables until it exits and never frees them, which
#  is bash's affair, not compass's. Anchored on bash's allocator and on frames
#  of the bash binary, neither of which compass's own stacks contain.
#
{
   bash allocations
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,definite,indirect,possible
   fun:malloc
   fun:xmalloc
   ...
}
{
   bash allocations through its own frames
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,definite,indirect,possible
   ...
   obj:/usr/bin/bash
   ...
}
#
#  The shell of the formatter tests
#
#  The format_files tests and the codegen end-to-end tests stand a shell script
#  in for the formatter. The script is not under /usr, so memcheck follows it
#  and runs the dash that interprets it, whose memory dash keeps until it
#  exits. Anchored on dash's allocators, ckmalloc and ckrealloc, on its
#  evaluator, evaltree, and on its setting of the working directory at start,
#  none of which compass's own stacks contain.
#
{
   dash allocations
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,definite,indirect,possible
   fun:*alloc
   fun:ck*alloc
   ...
}
{
   dash allocations of the commands it evaluates
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,definite,indirect,possible
   ...
   fun:evaltree
   ...
}
{
   dash working directory
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable,definite,indirect,possible
   fun:realloc
   fun:getcwd
   fun:getcwd
   fun:getpwd
   fun:setpwd
   ...
}
#
#  PCRE2's JIT
#
#  The compiled patterns run as machine code PCRE2 writes at run time, which
#  belongs to no object and keeps its own stack, so memcheck cannot unwind it
#  and names every frame ???. Its matcher reads the subject 16 bytes at a time,
#  past the end of the string within the aligned block, and branches on the
#  bytes beyond it, then discards them; the reads never cross a page. Anchored
#  on the first frame being one of no object: every frame of compass's code
#  names its binary, whose path is longer than three characters, so ??? never
#  matches it.
#
{
   PCRE2 JIT reads past the subject
   Memcheck:Addr16
   obj:???
}
{
   PCRE2 JIT branches on the bytes past the subject
   Memcheck:Cond
   obj:???
}
#
#  libstdc++'s emergency exception pool
#
#  libstdc++ allocates a pool at start for the exceptions it throws when the
#  heap is exhausted, and keeps it for the life of the process. Valgrind frees
#  it at exit through a hook of the shared libstdc++, but the Linux compass
#  binary links libstdc++ statically, so the pool is still reachable. Anchored
#  on the pool's own initialiser.
#
{
   libstdc++ emergency exception pool
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:malloc
   fun:_GLOBAL__sub_I_eh_alloc.cc
   ...
}
